   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the election law, in relation to withdrawal from a run-off election after a primary",
    "basePrintNo": "S7980",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7980-2009",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §6-162, El L",
    "lawSection": "Election Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S7980\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act\nto amend the election law, in relation to withdrawal from a\nrun-off election after a primary\n\nGENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nProvides a process for withdrawal from a primary election run-off by a\nqualifying candidate within three days after the initial primary.\n\nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 6-162 of the election law is amended by adding a new\nsubdivision 2 which provides that, in any jurisdiction authorizing a\nprimary run-off election, where one of the candidates eligible to\nparticipate in a run-off election officially withdraws from such\nelection by submitting a certificate of withdrawal to the local board\nwithin three days of the primary election, no such run-off election\nshall take place.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nUnder the current law, for the offices of NYC Mayor, Comptroller and\nPublic Advocate, if no candidate receives 40% or more of the votes in\nthe primary, the law requires a run-off primary election be held\nbetween the two candidates receiving the highest number of votes. The\nprovisions of the current law, with language that reads \"...the city\nshall conduct a run-off primary election...\" provide no mechanism or\nprocedure for withdrawal by one of the candidates even if such\ncandidate should choose to concede to the other. This major flaw in\nthe current law could force a primary election to be held at the cost\nof millions of dollars to taxpayers with only a single real\ncontestant. This would have been the case in the NYC 2005 primary\nelection had the ultimate winner failed to reach the threshold 40% of\nvotes after recanvassing.\n\nThis bill will correct this flaw and provide a convenient process and\nmechanism for legal withdrawal after voluntary concession by one of\nthe candidates who qualifies for a run-off in NYC or any other\njurisdiction that may enact a run-off provision.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\nNew Bill.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\nUnknown.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the thirtieth day after becoming a law.\n\n",
